            <tr><td><strong>Amsterdam</strong></td><td>92 days</td><td>4.1</td><td>13 (0.7%)</td><td>Highly restricted</td></td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="insight-box">
        <strong>Regulatory Signal:</strong> Amsterdam's 92-day average availability and Barcelona's 38.5% of listings with 30+ minimum nights suggest significant regulatory constraints. Paris's 43.9 average minimum nights (highest across all cities) indicates strong market orientation toward longer-term stays.
    </div>
</section>

<section id="takeaways">
    <h2>6. Key Takeaways & Strategic Implications</h2>

    <div class="takeaway">
        <span class="number">1</span>
        <h4>Premium Pricing Markets Carry Higher Entry Barriers</h4>
        <p>Bangkok's $1,400 median price is <span class="stat">10× Rome's $131</span>, reflecting a fundamentally different market segment. Investors entering Bangkok should plan for higher per-unit investment and potentially longer payback periods, while Rome offers accessible entry for budget-conscious operators.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="takeaway">
        <span class="number">2</span>
        <h4>Host Concentration Predicts Market Maturity</h4>
        <p>Bangkok (60.4%) and Barcelona (58.3%) professional host shares indicate <span class="stat">institutionalized markets</span> with established operating models. Amsterdam (6.8%) remains fragmented, presenting opportunities for individual hosts but also vulnerability to regulatory shifts.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="takeaway">
        <span class="number">3</span>
        <h4>Data Gaps Limit Paris/Sydney Analysis</h4>
